<DocsLayout title="Merging accounts">
  <h1>Merging accounts</h1>
  <p>
    A person can end up with two accounts in one project. One example is a
    Gmail sign-up (with the address <code>bob-at-gmail.com@accounts.example.com</code>)
    alongside a username account <code>bob</code> (<code>bob@accounts.example.com</code>).
    A merge joins them under one account <strong>without deleting
    anything</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>What a merge does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Whoever asks chooses the <strong>survivor</strong>; the server never
      picks. The other account is <strong>retired</strong>: it becomes
      <code>USER_STATUS_DEACTIVATED</code>, and <code>merged_into_user_id</code>
      names the survivor. All its sessions are revoked, it never signs in again,
      and it can never be reactivated: <code>ReactivateUser</code> refuses it,
      and SCIM no longer sees it. Its row, and everything stored under its id,
      stays.
    </li>
    <li>
      Everything that lets the person sign in the way they did moves to the
      survivor, in one transaction:
      <ul>
        <li>the retired account's <strong>linked providers</strong> (Google, Apple, …), always;</li>
        <li>its <strong>username</strong>, <strong>password</strong> and <strong>email</strong>, each only where the survivor has none of its own (a password that two-step verification protected does not move), so <code>bob</code> now signs in to the survivor;</li>
        <li>with <code>take_address</code>, its account address. The survivor's old address moves to the retired account, so neither is freed for someone else.</li>
      </ul>
      <strong>Passkeys do not move.</strong> A passkey is bound to the account
      it was registered for, so it would not sign in to the survivor. Register
      passkeys again on the survivor.
      If either account changes while the merge runs (another merge, a
      deactivation), nothing is written and the call answers
      <code>ABORTED</code>; try again.
    </li>
    <li>
      An email goes to every address either account had, saying which account
      was merged into which, so someone who did not ask for it hears at once.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <code>user.merged</code> event names the retired account and carries
      <code>merged_into_user_id</code>. Applications move whatever they hold
      under the retired id to the survivor.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Turning it on</h2>
  <p>
    Self-service merging is <strong>off</strong> until you set
    <code>GATEWAY_ACCOUNT_MERGE_ENABLED=true</code>. A merge retires an
    account for good, and applications learn about it only through the
    <code>user.merged</code> webhook, so merging relies on webhooks: turn
    them on (<code>GATEWAY_WEBHOOKS_ENABLED</code>) and subscribe every
    application that stores data under user ids to <code>user.merged</code>
    first. The admin <code>MergeUsers</code> works whether or not
    self-service merging is on, and it too announces each merge only through
    <code>user.merged</code>, so it needs webhooks as well. Identity logs
    <code>account_merge_without_webhooks</code> at boot when self-service
    merging is on and webhooks are off; no warning covers admin merges.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can merge</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The person</strong>, with <code>MergeAccounts</code> called from the
      survivor's session, with the client assurance a password sign-in needs.
      The session must come from a <strong>recent sign-in</strong>: within
      <code>GATEWAY_ACCOUNT_MERGE_REAUTH_MAX_AGE_SECONDS</code> (default 300,
      at most 3600)
      of the call, by any method the account has, its second factor
      included. A refresh does not count, nor does a session opened by a QR
      handoff, an anonymous sign-in or a password just added to an anonymous
      account: sign the person in on that device with one of their
      credentials. An older session gets
      <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> with
      <code>reauthentication_required</code>: sign the person in again, then
      retry. A stolen or long-lived session therefore cannot pull another
      account's credentials onto the survivor. The survivor is checked first,
      so an account that could not take another in learns nothing about the
      other's password. They prove they control the other account with its
      identifier (email or username) and password, checked by the same code as
      a password sign-in: the access mode, the lockout, the account's status,
      the verified-email requirement and the login policy all apply, with the
      same refusals and audit entries, on the per-IP login limit. An account
      protected by a second factor (enrolled two-step, or a policy requiring
      one) cannot be merged by password alone: turn two-step off there first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An admin</strong>, with <code>MergeUsers</code>, naming both
      accounts. No password is asked.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What cannot be merged</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An account into itself.</li>
    <li>An account that is not active.</li>
    <li>An anonymous account: upgrade it instead.</li>
    <li>An agent account. When its owner is merged away, its agents move to the survivor.</li>
    <li>A managed child's account, which stays with its guardian.</li>
    <li>An account that is a guardian: move its children first.</li>
    <li>
      In self-service, an account your identity provider manages (SCIM
      provisioned): merging it away would move its sign-in out of the provider's
      control. An admin can merge it.
    </li>
    <li>An admin's own account, by that admin.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These refusals answer <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>.</p>

  <h2>Two accounts for one mailbox</h2>
  <p>
    Every address is stored in canonical form (lower case, no
    <code>+tag</code>, no Gmail dots). An older release could store another
    spelling, such as <code>first.last@gmail.com</code>. Such an account is
    found only by that spelling, so signing in with
    <code>firstlast@gmail.com</code> by code or password created a second
    account. Each sign-in now moves its account's stored email to the
    canonical form, unless another live account already holds it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>identity repair-emails</code> handles the rest, once, across every
    project, deciding per mailbox. Without arguments it is a dry run that logs
    what it would do, by account id. With <code>--apply</code> it does exactly
    that: it rewrites each email whose canonical form is free, and merges two
    active accounts of one Gmail mailbox when both have a verified email,
    neither is managed by an identity provider and exactly one is linked to a
    sign-in provider (such as Google), keeping that one. Its merges follow
    every rule on this page, are audited, notify the person and emit
    <code>user.merged</code>, which the run delivers before it exits.
    Anything else is reported with the reason and left for
    <code>MergeUsers</code>: an unverified account (it may not be the
    mailbox owner's), a <code>+tag</code> difference outside Gmail (it may be
    another person's mailbox), more than two accounts, an inactive account,
    both or neither linked to a provider, or a pair this page's rules refuse.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
